# Patch 0053 — panfrost stops mapping imported buffers cacheable.
#
#   KERNEL_CONFIG=nocache build/build.sh kernel
#     -> build/out/h713-kernel-nocache.fit
#
# Like dispiommu.config, this fragment deliberately changes NOTHING. It exists
# only so the build gets its own tree and its own FIT name and cannot overwrite
# the production one. 0053 is in `series`, so the change itself is already in
# any build; this is purely about not clobbering h713-kernel.fit while it is
# still being evaluated on hardware.
#
# It does NOT set CONFIG_LOCALVERSION, for the same reason dispiommu.config
# does not: `uname -r` stays 6.18.38, so /lib/modules/6.18.38 -- the in-tree
# drivers AND the out-of-tree aic8800 WiFi/BT modules -- loads unchanged, the
# network stays up, and the DRM card numbering stays as production (card0
# panfrost, card1 AFBD). Confirm which kernel booted from the build date in
# `dmesg | head -1`, not from `uname -r`.
#
# WHAT IT TESTS. The GPU arm of tools/video/soak-display-only.sh panics in
# 40-90 s on the stock kernel; the VO=drm arm ran 40 minutes clean. 0053 removes
# a real memory-attribute mismatch on exactly the buffers those two arms differ
# over -- the scanout dumb buffers that mesa allocates on card1 (drm_gem_dma ->
# dma_alloc_wc, non-cacheable) and imports into panfrost, which mapped them
# write-back.
#
#   * GPU arm now survives well past 90 s -> 0053 is at least part of it.
#   * GPU arm still panics on the old schedule -> the mismatch was real and is
#     now fixed, but it is not the corruption. That is still a result: it
#     removes a variable, and the patch is worth keeping on its own terms.
#
# The second outcome is the one to expect. Mismatched cacheability explains
# torn or stale pixels; it does not obviously explain a write landing on a
# task_struct. Run it because it is cheap and it is correct either way, not
# because it is likely to be the answer.
CONFIG_LOCALVERSION=""
